+---
+status: accepted
+---
+
+# Validate against a supported keyword set, without depending on `jsonschema`
+
+Table validation (#4821) needs JSON Schema as its constraint language, but every
+runtime dependency `sqlite-utils` has today is a pure-Python wheel, and
+`jsonschema` transitively requires `rpds-py` — a compiled Rust extension. Taking
+it would make this the first release of `sqlite-utils` that can fail to install
+for want of a toolchain, in a tool whose whole appeal is that data people can
+`pip install` it anywhere. So we evaluate a **supported keyword set** of Draft
+2020-12 in-repo (`type` including unions with `null`, `properties`, `required`,
+`enum`, `minimum`, `maximum`, `minLength`, `maxLength`, `pattern`,
+`additionalProperties`) and add no runtime dependency.
+
+## Considered Options
+
+- **Depend on `jsonschema`.** Correct and complete, but +4 packages including a
+  compiled one. Rejected on install-story grounds.
+- **Optional extra (`pip install sqlite-utils[validate]`).** Keeps the base
+  install clean, but makes the CLI's behaviour depend on how it was installed —
+  the worst of both, and the schemas we need to support don't need the full
+  implementation anyway.
+- **Own implementation of a keyword set.** Chosen.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- **Unsupported keywords are a hard error, never a silent skip.** A schema using
+  `$ref`, `allOf`, `items`, `format`, … is rejected with a message naming the
+  keyword. This is the load-bearing part of the decision: partially validating a
+  schema and reporting "clean" is worse than refusing, because it manufactures
+  false confidence in dirty data. Product sign-off (#4821) made this an explicit
+  acceptance condition.
+- The supported set is documented for users, not just inferable from the source.
+- Rows are flat maps of column to SQLite scalar, so the keywords we skip are
+  mostly ones with nothing to act on (nested objects, arrays, composition). If
+  that stops being true, revisit — reversing this means either growing a real
+  validator or taking the dependency after all, and by then schemas in the wild
+  will depend on our behaviour.
diff --git a/docs/adr/0002-validate-exit-codes.md b/docs/adr/0002-validate-exit-codes.md
