+++ b/docs/adr/0002-validate-exit-codes.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+---
+status: accepted
+---
+
+# `sqlite-utils validate` distinguishes "dirty data" from "broken command" by exit code
+
+Every other `sqlite-utils` command signals failure the same way — raise
+`click.ClickException`, exit 1 — because for those commands there is only one
+kind of failure. `validate` has two, and its whole purpose is to be run from a
+pipeline: "the data has violations" (a normal, expected outcome worth failing a
+job over) and "I could not validate at all" (missing table, unreadable or
+unsupported schema file). Collapsing both into exit 1 would make it impossible to
+tell a data-quality gate from a broken invocation. So `validate` uses **0 = no
+violations, 1 = violations found, 2 = could not validate**, matching the
+convention `grep` and `diff` established and the code Click already uses for
+usage errors.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Operational failures inside `validate` raise a `ClickException` subclass that
+  sets `exit_code = 2`, rather than the bare `ClickException` used elsewhere in
+  `cli.py`. A reader comparing `validate` to its neighbours will notice the
+  difference; that is what this record is for.
+- Reversing it silently breaks callers' pipelines, which is exactly the class of
+  decision worth writing down.
