+---
+status: accepted
+---
+
+# The HTML report's UI is a TypeScript bundle, built by Vite and committed to the repo
+
+The HTML report has real interaction — filter by column, filter by violation
+kind, search, sort, expand a row for expected-vs-actual — and a hand-written
+template string inside `cli.py` would put that logic somewhere no test can reach
+and nobody dares edit (the failure mode #4821 was explicitly written to prevent).
+So the UI lives in `frontend/` as TypeScript, is built by Vite into a single
+IIFE bundle, and is tested by vitest against a real DOM. Python's only job is to
+inline that bundle plus the validation result into one file.
+
+The build output is **committed** to `sqlite_utils/report/static/` and shipped as
+package data.
+
+## Considered Options
+
+- **Build at install time.** Would need Node in the build environment of a
+  Python package that today builds with plain setuptools. Rejected — it makes
+  `pip install sqlite-utils` require a JS toolchain, the same failure mode as
+  ADR-0001.
+- **Build at report time.** Same problem, at a worse moment.
+- **Commit the bundle.** Chosen. `pip install` needs nothing new, `pytest` runs
+  with no network and no Node, and the source of truth is still TypeScript.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A generated artifact is under version control. Anyone changing `frontend/`
+  must rebuild (`npm --prefix frontend run build`) and commit the result, or the
+  shipped report won't match the source. A test asserts the committed bundle is
+  present and non-empty; CI for the JS side is `npm test` + `tsc --noEmit`.
+- Diffs on the bundle are noise. That is the price of the two properties above,
+  and it is paid by exactly one file.
+- The report must work from `file://` with no network, so the bundle is inlined,
+  never referenced — no CDN, no external font, no remote source map.
