index 2e506dd..5b45c70 100644
--- a/docs/cli.rst
+++ b/docs/cli.rst
@@ -1074,6 +1074,162 @@ The ``most_common`` and ``least_common`` columns will contain nested JSON arrays
         ["Condarco", 4288]
     ]
 
+.. _cli_validate:
+
+Validating data against a schema
+================================
+
+The ``sqlite-utils validate`` command checks every row in a table against a
+**row schema** - a `JSON Schema <https://json-schema.org/>`__ document describing
+what one row of that table should look like:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db creatures schema.json
+
+The schema is an object schema whose ``properties`` describe the table's columns:
+
+.. code-block:: json
+
+    {
+        "type": "object",
+        "properties": {
+            "id": {"type": "integer"},
+            "name": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
+            "age": {"type": ["integer", "null"], "minimum": 0},
+            "status": {"enum": ["active", "retired"]}
+        },
+        "required": ["id", "name"]
+    }
+
+Output looks like this:
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    creatures: 2 rows checked
+      3 violations in 2 rows
+
+      By column:
+        2: age
+        1: status
+
+      By kind:
+        1: enum
+        1: minimum
+        1: type-coercible
+
+The command exits **0** when the data is clean, **1** when there are violations,
+and **2** when the table could not be validated at all - a missing table, an
+unreadable schema file, or a schema using a keyword that is not supported. That
+lets a pipeline tell dirty data apart from a broken invocation:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db creatures schema.json || echo "Do not ship this"
+
+.. _cli_validate_keywords:
+
+Supported JSON Schema keywords
+------------------------------
+
+``sqlite-utils`` evaluates a deliberately small part of JSON Schema, and
+**rejects any schema using a keyword outside it** rather than skipping that
+keyword silently - a schema that is only partly understood would report clean
+data that is not.
+
+At the top level of the document:
+
+``type``
+    Must be ``"object"`` if present - each row is an object.
+``properties``
+    Maps column names to their constraints.
+``required``
+    Column names that must exist in the table. Note that a column which exists
+    is present on every row, so ``required`` only fails when the table has no
+    such column at all.
+``additionalProperties``
+    ``false`` reports any column the schema does not mention. Defaults to
+    ``true``, which ignores them.
+
+For each column:
+
+``type``
+    One of ``string``, ``integer``, ``number``, ``boolean``, ``null``, or a list
+    of them such as ``["string", "null"]``.
+``enum``
+    The permitted values.
+``minimum``, ``maximum``
+    Bounds, applied to numeric values.
+``minLength``, ``maxLength``
+    Bounds on the length of string values.
+``pattern``
+    A regular expression, searched (not anchored) against string values.
+
+Annotation keywords - ``$schema``, ``$id``, ``title``, ``description``,
+``default``, ``examples`` and friends - are accepted and ignored. Everything
+else, including ``$ref``, ``allOf``, ``anyOf``, ``oneOf``, ``items``, ``const``,
+``format`` and ``multipleOf``, is an error.
+
+.. _cli_validate_types:
+
+How SQLite values are typed
+---------------------------
+
+SQLite is dynamically typed and CSV imports arrive as text, so ``type`` is where
+most violations show up:
+
+- A ``NULL`` is the JSON value ``null`` on a field that exists. It fails
+  ``type`` unless the schema admits null, and it never fails ``required``. Say
+  ``"type": ["string", "null"]`` for a column that is allowed to be empty.
+- The empty string is an ordinary string. Pass ``--empty-null`` to treat it as
+  ``null`` instead, which matches ``sqlite-utils insert --empty-null``.
+- A value of the wrong type whose text would convert cleanly - the text
+  ``"123"`` against ``"type": "integer"`` - is reported as the
+  ``type-coercible`` kind, so type drift can be filtered apart from genuine
+  garbage. Pass ``--coerce`` to accept those values instead; later constraints
+  are then checked against the converted value.
+- SQLite has no boolean storage class, so ``"type": "boolean"`` accepts the
+  integers ``0`` and ``1``.
+- A ``BLOB`` satisfies no JSON Schema type.
+
+.. _cli_validate_reports:
+
+Reports
+-------
+
+``--json`` writes a machine-readable report, and ``--html`` writes a single
+self-contained HTML file - no network access needed - that can be sent to
+somebody who needs to review the data:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db creatures schema.json \
+      --json report.json --html report.html
+
+The HTML report lists the violations with filters by column and by violation
+kind, a search box, sortable columns, and an expandable detail view showing what
+was expected against what was actually stored. Neither report contains absolute
+filesystem paths.
+
+.. _cli_validate_large_tables:
+
+Large tables
+------------
+
+The whole table is scanned so that the counts are exact, but only the first
+1000 violations are listed in detail; the reports say how many were found in
+total. Use ``--max-violations`` to change that limit.
+
+``--limit`` is different: it stops the scan after N rows, for a quick probe of a
+very large table.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate data.db creatures schema.json --limit 1000
+
+Use ``--full-rows`` to include the entire row alongside each violation instead of
+just the primary key and the offending value.
+
