index 43b734d..d751514 100644
--- a/docs/python-api.rst
+++ b/docs/python-api.rst
@@ -1550,6 +1550,105 @@ And returns a ``ColumnDetails`` named tuple with the following fields:
 ``least_common``
     The ``N`` least common values as a list of ``(value, count)`` tuples`, or ``None`` if the table is entirely distinct or if the number of distinct values is less than N (since they will already have been returned in ``most_common``)
 
+.. _python_api_validate:
+
+Validating a table against a row schema
+=======================================
+
+The ``table.validate(schema)`` method checks every row in a table against a
+**row schema** - a `JSON Schema <https://json-schema.org/>`_ document describing
+what one row should look like. It is used by the
+:ref:`validate <cli_validate>` CLI command.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    result = db["creatures"].validate({
+        "type": "object",
+        "properties": {
+            "id": {"type": "integer"},
+            "name": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
+            "age": {"type": ["integer", "null"], "minimum": 0},
+        },
+    })
+    if not result.ok:
+        for violation in result.violations:
+            print(violation.key, violation.column, violation.kind)
+
+``schema`` is the already-parsed JSON Schema document, as a Python dictionary -
+reading the file is the caller's job.
+
+Only a documented set of keywords is evaluated. A schema using anything else
+raises ``sqlite_utils.validation.SchemaError`` **before any row is read**, rather
+than silently skipping the keyword - see :ref:`cli_validate_keywords` for the
+full list and the reasoning.
+
+It takes the following options:
+
+``scan_limit``
+    Only read this many rows, for a quick probe of a very large table. Defaults
+    to ``None``, meaning scan everything so that the counts are exact.
+
+``detail_cap``
+    Retain at most this many violations in full detail. Defaults to 1000. The
+    counts are unaffected.
+
+``coerce``
+    If ``True``, accept values whose text would convert cleanly to the expected
+    type - the text ``"123"`` for an ``integer``. Later constraints are then
+    checked against the converted value. Defaults to ``False``.
+
+``empty_is_null``
+    If ``True``, treat the empty string as ``null``. Defaults to ``False``,
+    which treats it as the ordinary string it is.
+
+``include_rows``
+    If ``True``, include the whole row alongside each violation. Defaults to
+    ``False``.
+
+It returns a ``ValidationResult`` with the following fields:
+
+``table``
+    The name of the table
+
+``rows_total``
+    The number of rows in the table
+
+``rows_scanned``
+    The number of rows actually read - the same as ``rows_total`` unless
+    ``scan_limit`` was used
+
+``rows_with_violations``
+    The number of rows with at least one violation
+
+``violation_count``
+    The total number of violations, which is exact even when ``detail_cap``
+    limited how many were retained
+
+``violations``
+    A list of ``Violation`` objects, at most ``detail_cap`` of them
+
+``counts_by_column``, ``counts_by_kind``
+    Dictionaries counting violations per column and per violation kind
+
+``mismatches``
+    A list of ``SchemaMismatch`` objects, one for each column the schema names
+    which the table does not have
+
+``ok``
+    ``True`` when there were no violations and no mismatches
+
+``truncated``
+    ``True`` when there were more violations than ``detail_cap`` retained
+
+Each ``Violation`` carries ``key`` (the row's primary key values, or its
+``rowid``), ``column``, ``kind`` (the JSON Schema keyword that failed, or
+``type-coercible``), ``expected``, ``actual``, ``actual_type`` (the SQLite
+storage class) and ``row``.
+
+Note that a SQL ``NULL`` is the JSON value ``null`` on a field that exists: it
+fails ``type`` unless the schema admits null, and never fails ``required``. See
+:ref:`cli_validate_types`.
+
 .. _python_api_add_column:
 
 Adding columns
